    #      EMPTY (ruling 6: no note for a directory with none), the list with
    #      mode 600, its rows counted by source.
    #   T2 the same with run-plain copied to run-plain-planted and one error row
    #      planted there (fx-b: outcome error, out_bytes empty, its output
    #      removed, as the runner leaves it): exit 0, stderr exactly one line,
    #      the note for 1 row of run-plain-planted (built from the source's
    #      own form), one corpus row fewer than T1, fx-b absent under that
    #      label.
    #   T2b the planted row in the --run directory instead (run-formula copied
    #      to run-formula-planted): the note names "host".
    #   T3 the runner, the clone's scripts/passthrough-run.sh at the phase
    #      commit, over the fixture corpus root rroot with the gate's DEBUG
    #      emend-copy and xlsx-diff copied, no --libreoffice, --ids selecting
    #      fa0000000001:
    #      T3a a fresh run directory: exit 0, one ok row;
    #      T3b a fresh run directory with fa0000000001/lo.host planted: exit 2,
    #          the earlier-run message from the source, no row, no output, the
    #          planted directory still there;
    #      T3c the same with lo-in.host;
    #      T3d only fa0000000001/lo.i686 planted, --label host: not refused,
    #          one ok row, lo.i686 left in place;
    #      T3e lo.host planted under fa0000000002, which --ids does not select:
    #          refused (every subdirectory counts, ruling 2).
    #   Every message and the note's form are read from the sources by grep,
    #   never typed; each pass check has a control that refuses another case's
    #   output.
    #
    # CARRIED TO M4 part 1 P1 from M3 part 7 P4's test script (printed in
    # records/logs/m3p7-p4/test.log), generated by
    # <home>/.cache/emend-build/m4p1-p1/gen/test.py with gen/p1_common.py.
    # P4's cases (the carried close-out scripts and their generator) are P4's
    # and are not rerun. P4's comment text follows below as the carried history;
    # the code is this phase's. What differs, and why:
    #  KA. The phase, the log directory and the subjects are M4 part 1 P1's: the
    #      base M3 part 7's close-out record commit, then the doc commit, the
    #      rules commit (specs/0001), the runner commit and the list commit,
    #      the phase commit, each by its full subject. The spec is specs/0030,
    #      pinned at its blob at the doc commit, 7bc47cae (FACTS.md); the
    #      working file, planning's rulings during P1, is printed and not
    #      asserted. The status pattern allows this phase's logs and
    #      planning's uncommitted specs/0030 alone.
    #  KB. No build. scripts/counting.sh and scripts/excel-check-list.py are
    #      read at the phase commit by git show into <scratch>/w; the runner is
    #      run from a clone of <repo> at the phase commit in <scratch>/w/clone
    #      (the runner finds its repository as its own directory's parent and
    #      reads its commit, Cargo.lock and rustc there, and sources
    #      scripts/lo-check.sh beside it); the gate's DEBUG emend-copy and
    #      xlsx-diff are copied from <target>/main/debug into <scratch>/w/bin
    #      and each copy's sha256 checked against the one line gate.log
    #      printed for it.
    #  KC. The fixtures, built under <scratch>/w/fx by a python3 -I program this
    #      script writes beside them (printed), all fictional. <scratch>/w, with
    #      the fixtures, the clone and the cases' outputs, is kept on the disk
    #      under <build>; this script removes no directory.
    #  KD. Root lines for the placeholders the carried history prints before a
    #      slash and this log never uses, each saying so.
    #
    # P4's text:
    # Milestone 3 part 7 P4 test.log (specs/0029-engineering-close.md, "P4:
    # the README and the list" as amended 2026-10-09, "The Excel check",
    # "Close-out push (after P4)" as amended, and "Planning rulings during P4
    # (2026-10-09)" rulings 3 and 4): the spec pinned by the hash of its
    # committed blob, planning's working copy printed; the phase's three
    # commits by role; T1 part 6's close-out push, ci-run and scan scripts,
    # copied to <home>/.cache/emend-build/m3p7-close/carried, each equal to the
    # text part 6's committed logs printed; T2 the counting library the
    # close-out sources, equal to the phase commit's scripts/counting.sh, and
    # its one line of difference from part 6's copy, which part 6's push.log
    # printed; T3 gen.py printed and run into a scratch directory, the three
    # scripts it writes equal to the ones on the disk; T4 each generated
    # script's diff against part 6's, printed in full; T5 bash -n, the three
    # shape greps with fixtures, and no non-ASCII byte, U+2014 or home path in
    # each generated script; T6 the Excel-record check's three functions and
    # its pre-mode section present once each, the base, record and tip
    # patterns resolved or read; this log runs no cargo; this script's own
    # stderr counted.
    #
    #
    # Usage: m5p6-p4-test.sh <phase commit> > <file outside the tree>; the
    # caller moves the output to records/logs/m5p6-p4/test.log.
    set -uo pipefail
    SELF_DIR=$(cd "$(dirname "$0")" && pwd)
    SELF=$SELF_DIR/$(basename "$0")
    # specs/0017 P1 verification ruling 7: this script's own standard error, kept and counted at the end.
    mkdir -p "$SELF_DIR/out" || exit 2
    ERR=$SELF_DIR/out/test.stderr
    exec 2>"$ERR"
    cd "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)" || exit 2
    MAIN=$(pwd)
    
    C=${1:?phase commit required}
    unset EMEND_CORPUS_DIR EMEND_KEEP_DIR RUSTDOCFLAGS RUSTFLAGS CARGO_TARGET_DIR
    export RUSTUP_AUTO_INSTALL=0 CARGO_TERM_COLOR=never
    PHASE_DIR=$HOME/.cache/emend-build/m5p6-p4
    BUILD_ROOT=${TEST_BUILD:-$PHASE_DIR/test}
    SUPERSEDED_DIR=$PHASE_DIR/superseded
    TARGET_DIR=$PHASE_DIR/target
    SPEC=specs/0037-measurement-and-close.md
    SPEC_WANT=09ad50a18fa73b2e2f237f8995d0fd321d10f2958c8e13cc59b5cf46fd351603
    mkdir -p "$BUILD_ROOT" || exit 2
    # Change QC: made on the disk under <build> and kept after the run.
    B=$(mktemp -d -p "$BUILD_ROOT") || exit 2
    git show "$C:scripts/counting.sh" >"$B/counting.sh" || exit 2
    . "$B/counting.sh"
    fail=0
    check() { echo "  $1: $2"; [ "$2" = yes ] || fail=1; }
    yn() { if "$@"; then echo yes; else echo no; fi; }
    # For a condition that must NOT hold: a leading bang inside yn runs a
    # command named by the bang (specs/0001), so a negation has its own helper.
    ynnot() { if "$@"; then echo no; else echo yes; fi; }
    
    head_is() { [ "$(git rev-parse HEAD)" = "$1" ]; }
    full_sha() { printf '%s' "$1" | LC_ALL=C "$G" -qE '^[0-9a-f]{40}$'; }
    # The given commit is the phase commit when it is a full sha and HEAD.
    given_ok() { full_sha "$1" && head_is "$1"; }
    if given_ok "$C"; then :; else echo "the phase commit must be a full 40-hex sha that is HEAD, not $C" >&2; exit 2; fi
    NULL_SHA=0000000000000000000000000000000000000000
    case "${C: -1}" in 0) LAST=1 ;; *) LAST=0 ;; esac
    FAKE_SHA=${C:0:39}$LAST
    SHORT_SHA=${C:0:7}
    # This phase's logs, untracked, and planning's modified spec (changes RA and
    # UA).
    OURS="^(\\?\\? records/logs/m5p6-p4/| M specs/0037-measurement-and-close[.]md\$)"
    entry_all=$(run_lines git status --porcelain)
    entry_ours=$(run_matches -E "$OURS" git status --porcelain)
    entry_clean() { [ "$entry_all" != error ] && [ "$entry_ours" != error ] && [ "$entry_all" = "$entry_ours" ]; }
    if entry_clean; then :; else
      echo "the working tree holds changes outside this phase's logs and planning's spec" >&2
      exit 2
    fi
    
    scrub() {
      sed -e "s#$MAIN#<repo>#g" -e "s#$SELF_DIR#<scripts>#g" -e "s#$B#<scratch>/w#g" \
        -e "s#$BUILD_ROOT#<build>#g" -e "s#$SUPERSEDED_DIR#<superseded>#g" \
        -e "s#$TARGET_DIR#<target>#g" -e "s#$PHASE_DIR#<phase>#g" \
        -e "s#$HOME#<home>#g"
    }
    exec 3>&1
    exec > >(scrub >&3)
    SCRUB_PID=$!
    
    echo "command: git rev-parse HEAD"
    echo "invocation: bash <scripts>/$(basename "$0") $*"
    echo "input: RERUN_NOTE: ${RERUN_NOTE:-unset}"
    echo "input: RERUN_FILES: ${RERUN_FILES:-unset}"
    echo "input: ANTHROPIC_API_KEY: $([ -n "${ANTHROPIC_API_KEY:-}" ] && echo set || echo unset) (its value never printed)"
    echo "input: HOME: set, read for the paths under it, printed as <home>"
    echo "input: TEST_BUILD: ${TEST_BUILD:-unset} (the build root, <build>)"
    echo "input: RUSTDOCFLAGS, RUSTFLAGS and CARGO_TARGET_DIR: unset by this script at entry; each cargo command below names its target directory"
    echo "about: M5 part 6 P4 phase commit $C; HEAD at run: $(git rev-parse HEAD)"
    echo
    echo "## the shape greps, the three and PYB, and the defined-helper check, over this script, before its log (specs/0001; ruling Z; change UF)"
    D='$'
    SHAPE_CHAIN="\\${D}\\((yn|ynnot|is|isnot) .*(&&|\\|\\|)"
    SHAPE_BANG="\\${D}\\((yn|ynnot|is|isnot) !"
    SHAPE_ASSIGN="\\${D}\\((yn|ynnot|is|isnot) [A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*="
    printf '%s\n' "x=${D}(is [ 1 ] && true)" "y=${D}(isnot ! true)" "z=${D}(yn N=1 true)" >"$B/shape-fixture"
    echo "command: count_matches -E <shape> <this script>, and the same over a fixture holding one line of each shape"
    for shape in CHAIN BANG ASSIGN; do
      var=SHAPE_$shape
      n_self=$(count_matches -E "${!var}" "$SELF")
      n_fix=$(count_matches -E "${!var}" "$B/shape-fixture")
      echo "  shape $shape: in this script $n_self; in the fixture $n_fix"
      check "$shape: this script holds none" "$(yn [ "$n_self" = 0 ])"
      check "control: $shape: the same form finds the fixture's one line" "$(yn [ "$n_fix" = 1 ])"
    done
    # Change UF: the fourth shape, PYB, P3's form: a line that is not a comment
    # and calls python3 (followed by whitespace, then a dash or a .py path)
    # without -I -B. The interpreter's name is held in a variable here, so no
    # line of this form spells a call.
    PY=python3
    PYB_CALL="^[^#]*$PY[[:space:]]+(-|[^[:space:]]*[.]py)"
    PYB_OK="$PY -I -B( |\$)"
    # pyb_calls <file> and pyb_without <file>: the calls, and the calls without
    # -I -B. They print, so their values are compared.
    pyb_calls() { count_matches -E "$PYB_CALL" "$1"; }
    pyb_without() { LC_ALL=C "$G" -E "$PYB_CALL" "$1" | LC_ALL=C "$G" -vE "$PYB_OK" | count_lines /dev/stdin; }
    printf '%s\n' "$PY -c x" "$PY -I -B -c x" "# $PY -c x" "command -v $PY" >"$B/shape-pyb-fixture"
    n_pyb_self=$(pyb_without "$SELF")
    n_pyb_fix=$(pyb_without "$B/shape-pyb-fixture")
    n_calls_self=$(pyb_calls "$SELF")
    n_calls_fix=$(pyb_calls "$B/shape-pyb-fixture")
    echo "  shape PYB: in this script $n_pyb_self (of its $n_calls_self Python calls); in the fixture $n_pyb_fix (of its $n_calls_fix)"
    check "PYB: this script holds none (change UF)" "$(yn [ "$n_pyb_self" = 0 ])"
    check "control: PYB: the same form finds the fixture's one call without -I -B, of its two calls, the comment and the command -v line not calls" "$(yn [ "$n_pyb_fix:$n_calls_fix" = 1:2 ])"
    check "control: the call form finds this script's 4 Python call lines, the defined-helper check's two runs and their two command lines (change UF)" "$(yn [ "$n_calls_self" = 4 ])"
    check "control: ynnot reports no on a passing command" "$(yn [ "$(ynnot true)" = no ])"
    # Change UF (ruling Z): the defined-helper check over this script's own
    # source, by the phase's gen/defined.py copied into <scratch>/w and checked
    # by its sha256, then over a planted script, the control.
    DEF_WANT=9338b772da9973dd3fabb6c3b33e4e3d8a8150bc3005666a2e3d8011eb0fb4ea
    cp "$PHASE_DIR/gen/defined.py" "$B/defined.py" </dev/null
    def_cp_rc=$?
    DEF_HAVE=$(sha256sum <"$B/defined.py" | cut -d' ' -f1)
    echo "command: cp <phase>/gen/defined.py <scratch>/w/defined.py: exit $def_cp_rc; sha256sum: $DEF_HAVE"
    def_copied() { [ "$def_cp_rc" = 0 ] && [ "$DEF_HAVE" = "$DEF_WANT" ]; }
    check "the copy is the defined-helper program this script was generated against, sha256 $DEF_WANT (change UF)" "$(yn def_copied)"
    echo "command: sed 's/^/    /' <scratch>/w/defined.py"
    sed 's/^/    /' "$B/defined.py"
    DEF_RUN='import importlib.util, sys; s = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location("defined", sys.argv[1]); d = importlib.util.module_from_spec(s); s.loader.exec_module(d); d.check(open(sys.argv[2]).read(), sys.argv[3])'
    echo "command: python3 -I -B -c <the loader: the program loaded from its path, its check run over the file> <scratch>/w/defined.py <this script> $(basename "$SELF")"
    python3 -I -B -c "$DEF_RUN" "$B/defined.py" "$SELF" "$(basename "$SELF")" >"$B/defined-self.out" 2>&1 </dev/null
    def_rc=$?
    sed 's/^/    /' "$B/defined-self.out"
    echo "  exit $def_rc"
    def_self_ok() { [ "$def_rc" = 0 ] && [ "$(count_lines "$B/defined-self.out")" = 4 ] && [ "$(count_matches -E ': yes$' "$B/defined-self.out")" = 4 ]; }
    check "every helper a yn or ynnot call of this script names is defined in it, yn and ynnot themselves are defined in it, and the program's two planted controls find their planted names (4 lines yes; rulings Z and AD; change UF)" "$(yn def_self_ok)"
    # The planted calls are built from pieces, so this text holds no call of an
    # undefined name.
    printf '%s\n' 'f() { :; }' "x=\$""(yn f)" "y=\$""(ynnot g)" >"$B/c-defined.sh"
    echo "command: python3 -I -B -c <the loader> <scratch>/w/defined.py <scratch>/w/c-defined.sh c-defined.sh (a planted script defining f and calling f and g)"
    python3 -I -B -c "$DEF_RUN" "$B/defined.py" "$B/c-defined.sh" c-defined.sh >"$B/c-defined.out" 2>&1 </dev/null
    cdef_rc=$?
    sed 's/^/    /' "$B/c-defined.out"
    echo "  exit $cdef_rc"
    def_ctl_ok() { [ "$cdef_rc" != 0 ] && [ "$(count_matches -F 'no, undefined: g' "$B/c-defined.out")" = 1 ]; }
    check "control: the same program names g undefined in the planted script and exits non-zero (ruling Z; change UF)" "$(yn def_ctl_ok)"
    
    echo
    echo "## the phase's commits by role, read from git"
    by_subject() { git log --format='%H' -n 1 -F --grep="$1"; }
    REC=$(by_subject 'M5 part 6 P3 record: the size and API records, the harness reruns, the logs, the check')
    DOC=$(by_subject "M5 part 6 P4: commit planning docs (the P3 verification and rulings)")
    RD1=$(by_subject "M5 part 6 P4: the README brought to Milestone 5")
    RDM=$(by_subject "M5 part 6 P4: every feature line cites the closing size record")
    echo "command: git log --format=%H -n 1 -F --grep=<the role's full subject>, for each role"
    echo "  the previous phase's record commit (the base): ${REC:-none}"
    echo "  the doc commit: ${DOC:-none}"
    echo "  the first code commit of the README: ${RD1:-none}"
    echo "  the second code commit of the README (ruling AI), the phase commit: ${RDM:-none}"
    echo "command: git rev-parse <phase commit>~0 to <phase commit>~3"
    for k in 0 1 2 3; do git rev-parse "$C~$k"; done | sed 's/^/    /'
    roles_in_order() {
      local r k=0
      for r in "$RDM" "$RD1" "$DOC" "$REC"; do
        [ -n "$r" ] || return 1
        [ "$(git rev-parse "$C~$k")" = "$r" ] || return 1
        k=$((k + 1))
      done
    }
    check "the two README commits, the doc commit and the base, each the next one's parent, the second README commit the phase commit (change UA; ruling AI)" "$(yn roles_in_order)"
    roles_ctl() { local RDM=$RD1; roles_in_order; }
    check "control: roles_in_order refuses the first README commit named as the phase commit" "$(ynnot roles_ctl)"
    roles_ctl2() { local DOC=$REC; roles_in_order; }
    check "control: roles_in_order refuses the base named as the doc commit" "$(ynnot roles_ctl2)"
    roles_ctl3() { local RD1=$DOC; roles_in_order; }
    check "control: roles_in_order refuses the doc commit named as the first README commit" "$(ynnot roles_ctl3)"
    echo
    echo "phase: Milestone 5 part 6 P4 ($SPEC) test.log; run at $(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)"
    if [ -n "${RERUN_NOTE:-}" ]; then
      echo "rerun: $RERUN_NOTE"
      echo "command: sha256sum and wc -c over each superseded attempt named above, with the directory it is kept in (ruling AA; change UG)"
      for sf in ${RERUN_FILES:-}; do
        if [ -f "$sf" ]; then
          printf '    %s  %s bytes  %s  kept in %s\n' "$(sha256sum "$sf" | cut -d' ' -f1)" "$(wc -c <"$sf")" "$(basename "$sf")" "$(dirname "$sf")"
        else
          printf '    NO HASH EXISTS for %s: the file is not there at run time (named in %s)\n' "$(basename "$sf")" "$(dirname "$sf")"
        fi
      done
      if [ -z "${RERUN_FILES:-}" ]; then echo "    RERUN_FILES was not set, so no attempt above is hashed by this run"; fi
    fi
    n_cargo=$(pgrep -x cargo | wc -l)
    n_rustc=$(pgrep -x rustc | wc -l)
    echo "SERIALIZED: no other log run was in flight; cargo processes at entry: $n_cargo; rustc processes at entry: $n_rustc; this log builds in <repo> into <target>/main, one cargo command at a time (change QB)"
    no_build_running() { [ "$((n_cargo + n_rustc))" = 0 ]; }
    check "no cargo or rustc process was running at entry" "$(yn no_build_running)"
    echo "root: <repo> stands for the repository's top level (inside <home>, so scrubbed first); scripts/counting.sh, the spec, the crate's sources named in block R and P3's test.log and gate.log are read from it by git show at the phase commit, the working spec is hashed in it, and cargo test, clippy and doc run in it with their output in <target>/main"
    echo "root: <home> stands for the home directory of the account that ran this script"
    echo "root: <phase> stands for <home>/.cache/emend-build/m5p6-p4, the phase's directory on the disk; <phase>/gen/defined.py, the defined-helper program, is copied from it (change UF)"
    echo "root: <scripts> stands for <phase>/scripts, this script's own directory on the disk, which holds the script and, in <scripts>/out, its own standard error (specs/0017 P1 verification ruling 7)"
    echo "root: <target> stands for <phase>/target, the phase's one target root (specs/0001); <target>/main holds this log's builds (cargo test on both targets, clippy, doc)"
    echo "root: <build> stands for the build root on the disk, <phase>/test unless TEST_BUILD names another"
    echo "root: <superseded> stands for <phase>/superseded, where a rerun's superseded files are kept; read only"
    echo "root: <scratch>/w stands for this run's mktemp -d working directory under <build>, kept on the disk after the run (change QC): <scratch>/w/counting.sh the phase commit's library, sourced; the captured cargo outputs (*.out); <scratch>/w/p3-test.log and <scratch>/w/p3-gate.log, P3's test.log and gate.log read at the phase commit; <scratch>/w/defined.py, the defined-helper program copied, and its outputs; the controls' fixtures (changes RD, UC, UF and UH)"
    
    apart() { case "$1/" in "$2"/*) return 1 ;; esac; case "$2/" in "$1"/*) return 1 ;; esac; return 0; }
    roots_distinct() { apart "$BUILD_ROOT" "$MAIN" && apart "$BUILD_ROOT" "$SELF_DIR" && apart "$MAIN" "$SELF_DIR" && apart "$SUPERSEDED_DIR" "$SELF_DIR" && apart "$SUPERSEDED_DIR" "$BUILD_ROOT" && apart "$SUPERSEDED_DIR" "$MAIN" && apart "$TARGET_DIR" "$BUILD_ROOT" && apart "$TARGET_DIR" "$MAIN"; }
    check "the roots other than <home> and <phase> are distinct, none inside another (<scratch>/w inside <build>)" "$(yn roots_distinct)"
    roots_ctl() { apart "$B" "$BUILD_ROOT"; }
    check "control: apart refuses <scratch>/w against <build>, which holds it" "$(ynnot roots_ctl)"
    echo "ANTHROPIC_API_KEY set in this shell: $([ -n "${ANTHROPIC_API_KEY:-}" ] && echo yes || echo no)"
    key_unset() { [ -z "${ANTHROPIC_API_KEY:-}" ]; }
    check "ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is not set" "$(yn key_unset)"
    key_set_ctl() { local ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=planted; key_unset; }
    check "control: the same check refuses a shell where the variable is set" "$(ynnot key_set_ctl)"
    echo "at entry: status lines $entry_all; of them this phase's logs or planning's spec $entry_ours; the lines:"
    git status --porcelain | sed 's/^/    /'
    echo "  the phase commit given to this script: $C"
    echo "  git rev-parse HEAD at this run:        $(git rev-parse HEAD)"
    echo "  the controls' commits: the null commit $NULL_SHA; the fabricated commit $FAKE_SHA (the given one, its last hex digit changed); the short sha $SHORT_SHA"
    check "the phase commit given to this script is a full sha" "$(yn full_sha "$C")"
    check "the phase commit given to this script is HEAD" "$(yn head_is "$C")"
    check "control: the same comparison refuses the null commit" "$(ynnot given_ok "$NULL_SHA")"
    check "control: the same comparison refuses a fabricated commit" "$(ynnot given_ok "$FAKE_SHA")"
    check "control: the same comparison refuses a short sha of the real commit" "$(ynnot given_ok "$SHORT_SHA")"
    short_names_head() { [ "$(git rev-parse "$SHORT_SHA")" = "$(git rev-parse HEAD)" ]; }
    check "control: the short sha is the real commit's, so only its form is refused" "$(yn short_names_head)"
    ours_ctl() { printf ' M scripts/check.sh\n' | LC_ALL=C "$G" -qE "$OURS"; }
    check "control: the phase's status pattern refuses a modified gate file" "$(ynnot ours_ctl)"
    ours_src_ctl() { printf ' M crates/emend/src/cells.rs\n' | LC_ALL=C "$G" -qE "$OURS"; }
    check "control: the phase's status pattern refuses a modified crate source" "$(ynnot ours_src_ctl)"
    ours_roadmap_ctl() { printf ' M ROADMAP.md\n' | LC_ALL=C "$G" -qE "$OURS"; }
    check "control: the phase's status pattern refuses a modified ROADMAP.md (committed and clean in this phase)" "$(ynnot ours_roadmap_ctl)"
    ours_rec_ctl() { printf '?? records/logs/m5p6-p4/gate.log\n?? records/logs/m5p6-p3/gate.log\n?? records/logs/m5p6-p4x/gate.log\n M records/logs/m5p6-p4/gate.log\n M ROADMAP.md\n M specs/0037-measurement-and-close.md\n?? specs/0037-measurement-and-close.md\n M specs/0036-charts-write-second-set.md\n?? records/size-m5p6-2026-01-01-%s.md\n' "$SHORT_SHA" | count_matches -E "$OURS" /dev/stdin; }
    check "control: the phase's status pattern takes this phase's untracked log and planning's modified specs/0037 alone, no other phase's log, no sibling directory, no tracked log, no untracked spec, no other spec and no record (changes RA and UA)" "$(yn [ "$(ours_rec_ctl)" = 2 ])"
    ours_readme_ctl() { printf ' M README.md\n' | LC_ALL=C "$G" -qE "$OURS"; }
    check "control: the phase's status pattern refuses a modified README.md (committed and clean in this phase; change UA)" "$(ynnot ours_readme_ctl)"
    SPEC_HAVE=$(sha256sum <"$SPEC" | cut -d' ' -f1)
    SPEC_BLOB=$(git show "$C:$SPEC" | sha256sum | cut -d' ' -f1)
    echo "command: git show <phase commit>:$SPEC | sha256sum: $SPEC_BLOB; sha256sum $SPEC (the working file, printed and not asserted: planning holds a later edit uncommitted; changes RA and UA): $SPEC_HAVE"
    check "the spec's blob at the phase commit is at the hash this log pins" "$(yn [ "$SPEC_BLOB" = "$SPEC_WANT" ])"
    spec_ctl() { [ "$(printf 'x' | sha256sum | cut -d' ' -f1)" = "$SPEC_WANT" ]; }
    check "control: the same comparison refuses the hash of another file" "$(ynnot spec_ctl)"
    echo
    echo "## script that wrote this log"
    sed 's/^/    /' "$SELF"
    echo
    echo "## the shared counting library this log sources"
    sed 's/^/    /' "$B/counting.sh"
    echo
    echo "## counting controls"
    counting_controls
    if [ "$counting_controls_ok" != yes ]; then fail=1; fi
    
    # failed_names <cargo output>: prints the name of every test the run failed,
    # from its FAILED lines, or "none" (part 6 P3's form). It prints, so it is
    # never a check's condition.
    failed_names() {
      local n
      n=$(LC_ALL=C "$G" -a -E '^test .* \.\.\. FAILED$' "$1" | sed -E 's/^test (.*) \.\.\. FAILED$/\1/')
      if [ -n "$n" ]; then printf '%s\n' "$n" | sed 's/^/    failed test: /'; else echo "    failed tests: none"; fi
    }
    printf 'test a::b ... ok\ntest a::c ... FAILED\n' >"$B/c-failed"
    check "control: failed_names prints the name of the one FAILED line of two" "$(yn [ "$(failed_names "$B/c-failed")" = "    failed test: a::c" ])"
    printf 'test a::b ... ok\n' >"$B/c-failed-none"
    check "control: failed_names prints none for a run with no FAILED line" "$(yn [ "$(failed_names "$B/c-failed-none")" = "    failed tests: none" ])"
    # diag_lines <cargo output>: the lines a warning or an error begins with
    # (rustc's and clippy's "warning:" / "warning[...]" / "error:" / "error[...]"),
    # counted by the counting library's form.
    DIAG='^(warning|error)(\[[A-Za-z0-9_:]+\])?: '
    diag_lines() { count_matches -E "$DIAG" "$1"; }
    printf 'warning: unused import\nerror[E0433]: failed to resolve\n    Finished x\nnote: this is not one\n' >"$B/c-diag"
    check "control: diag_lines counts the two diagnostic lines of a four-line fixture" "$(yn [ "$(diag_lines "$B/c-diag")" = 2 ])"
    
    CT=$TARGET_DIR/main
    SETS="off charts images charts,images charts,read all"
    # set_args <set>: the cargo arguments of a feature set.
    set_args() { case $1 in off) ;; all) echo "--all-features" ;; *) echo "--features $1" ;; esac; }
    # test_run <name> <cargo args...>: runs cargo with the args into <target>/main,
    # keeps the output as <name>.out, prints the exit, the summary lines and the
    # failed names, and writes <name>.rc, .passed, .failed and .nok. It prints,
    # so it is never a check's condition; run_passed reads its files.
    test_run() {
      local name=$1 rc passed failed nok
      shift
      echo "command: CARGO_TARGET_DIR=<target>/main cargo $*"
      CARGO_TARGET_DIR=$CT cargo "$@" >"$B/$name.out" 2>&1 </dev/null
      rc=$?
      LC_ALL=C "$G" -a -E '^test result: ' "$B/$name.out" | sed 's/^/    /'
      failed_names "$B/$name.out"
      passed=$(awk '/^test result: / { for (i = 1; i < NF; i++) if ($(i + 1) ~ /^passed;$/) n += $i } END { print n + 0 }' "$B/$name.out")
      failed=$(awk '/^test result: / { for (i = 1; i < NF; i++) if ($(i + 1) ~ /^failed;$/) n += $i } END { print n + 0 }' "$B/$name.out")
      nok=$(count_matches -E '^test .* \.\.\. ok$' "$B/$name.out")
      echo "$rc" >"$B/$name.rc"; echo "$passed" >"$B/$name.passed"; echo "$failed" >"$B/$name.failed"; echo "$nok" >"$B/$name.nok"
      echo "  $name: exit $rc; passed $passed; failed $failed; executed (passed plus failed) $((passed + failed)); test lines ending ok $nok"
    }
    # run_passed <name>: exit 0, failed 0, passed more than 0 and equal to the ok lines.
    run_passed() {
      local rc passed failed nok
      rc=$(cat "$B/$1.rc"); passed=$(cat "$B/$1.passed"); failed=$(cat "$B/$1.failed"); nok=$(cat "$B/$1.nok")
      [ "$rc" = 0 ] && [ "$failed" = 0 ] && [ "$passed" -gt 0 ] && [ "$passed" = "$nok" ]
    }
    echo 0 >"$B/c-run.rc"; echo 3 >"$B/c-run.passed"; echo 1 >"$B/c-run.failed"; echo 3 >"$B/c-run.nok"
    check "control: run_passed refuses a run with one failed test" "$(ynnot run_passed c-run)"
    echo 0 >"$B/c-run2.rc"; echo 3 >"$B/c-run2.passed"; echo 0 >"$B/c-run2.failed"; echo 2 >"$B/c-run2.nok"
    check "control: run_passed refuses a run whose passed count is not its ok lines" "$(ynnot run_passed c-run2)"
    echo 0 >"$B/c-run3.rc"; echo 3 >"$B/c-run3.passed"; echo 0 >"$B/c-run3.failed"; echo 3 >"$B/c-run3.nok"
    check "control: run_passed takes a clean run of three" "$(yn run_passed c-run3)"
    
    echo
    echo "## W: cargo test --workspace on both targets (the gate's own test steps)"
    test_run ws-host test --workspace
    check "W host: exit 0, none failed, some ran, passed equals the ok lines" "$(yn run_passed ws-host)"
    test_run ws-i686 test --workspace --target i686-unknown-linux-musl
    check "W i686: exit 0, none failed, some ran, passed equals the ok lines" "$(yn run_passed ws-i686)"
    
    echo
    echo "## F: cargo test -p emend with each feature set the gate's clippy and doc steps use, on both targets (P3's six; change UB)"
    for target in host i686; do
      for set in $SETS; do
        args=(test -p emend)
        [ "$target" = host ] || args+=(--target i686-unknown-linux-musl)
        # shellcheck disable=SC2207
        args+=($(set_args "$set"))
        test_run "f-$target-$set" "${args[@]}"
        check "F $target, features $set: exit 0, none failed, some ran, passed equals the ok lines" "$(yn run_passed "f-$target-$set")"
      done
    done
    more_with_all() { [ "$(cat "$B/f-$1-all.passed")" -gt "$(cat "$B/f-$1-off.passed")" ]; }
    check "F host: the all-features run passes more tests than the run with none (the sets differ)" "$(yn more_with_all host)"
    check "F i686: the all-features run passes more tests than the run with none" "$(yn more_with_all i686)"
    more_ctl() { [ "$(cat "$B/f-host-off.passed")" -gt "$(cat "$B/f-host-all.passed")" ]; }
    check "control: the same comparison refuses the reverse order" "$(ynnot more_ctl)"
    
    echo
    echo "## E: each W and F run's passed count against P3's committed test.log and gate.log (the README changes no compiled file; change UC)"
    P3TEST=records/logs/m5p6-p3/test.log
    P3GATE=records/logs/m5p6-p3/gate.log
    P3FORM='^  passed: host off [0-9]+, '
    echo "command: git show <phase commit>:$P3TEST > <scratch>/w/p3-test.log; git show <phase commit>:$P3GATE > <scratch>/w/p3-gate.log; grep -E '$P3FORM' <scratch>/w/p3-gate.log"
    git show "$C:$P3TEST" >"$B/p3-test.log"
    rc_p3t=$?
    git show "$C:$P3GATE" >"$B/p3-gate.log"
    rc_p3g=$?
    n_p3line=$(count_matches -E "$P3FORM" "$B/p3-gate.log")
    P3LINE=$(LC_ALL=C "$G" -E "$P3FORM" "$B/p3-gate.log")
    echo "  exits $rc_p3t and $rc_p3g; the gate log's matching lines $n_p3line:"
    printf '%s\n' "$P3LINE" | sed 's/^/    /'
    p3_read() { [ "$rc_p3t" = 0 ] && [ "$rc_p3g" = 0 ] && [ "$n_p3line" = 1 ]; }
    check "P3's test.log and gate.log were read at the phase commit, the gate log holding exactly one summary line" "$(yn p3_read)"
    # p3_label <set>: P3's gate log's label for a feature set of this log (its
    # summary line names them off, ch, im, ci, cr and all).
    p3_label() { case $1 in off) echo off ;; charts) echo ch ;; images) echo im ;; charts,images) echo ci ;; charts,read) echo cr ;; all) echo all ;; *) echo none ;; esac; }
    # p3_count <line> <target> <label>: the line's "passed: " and its closing
    # parenthesis dropped, its comma-separated fields "<target> <label> <n>"
    # read; the figure when exactly one field names that target and label, else
    # "none". It prints, so its value is compared, never run as a condition.
    p3_count() {
      local hits
      hits=$(printf '%s\n' "$1" | sed -E 's/^  passed: //; s/ [(].*$//' | tr ',' '\n' | sed 's/^ //' | LC_ALL=C "$G" -E "^$2 $3 [0-9]+$" | awk '{ print $3 }')
      if [ -n "$hits" ] && [ "$(printf '%s\n' "$hits" | wc -l)" = 1 ]; then echo "$hits"; else echo none; fi
    }
    # p3t_count <log> <run name>: the passed figure of the log's one line
    # "  <run name>: exit 0; passed <n>; failed 0; ...", P3's test log's form for
    # a run this log repeats under the same name; "none" when not exactly one
    # line reads so. It prints, so its value is compared.
    p3t_count() {
      local hits
      hits=$(LC_ALL=C "$G" -E "^  $2: exit 0; passed [0-9]+; failed 0; " "$1" | sed -E 's/^  [^:]*: exit 0; passed ([0-9]+); .*$/\1/')
      if [ -n "$hits" ] && [ "$(printf '%s\n' "$hits" | wc -l)" = 1 ]; then echo "$hits"; else echo none; fi
    }
    # same_count <a> <b>: both are the same decimal number.
    same_count() { printf '%s' "$1" | LC_ALL=C "$G" -qE '^[0-9]+$' && [ "$1" = "$2" ]; }
    CTL_LINE='  passed: host off 10, host ch 12, host im 11, i686 off 20, i686 ci 21, i686 ci 22'
    check "control: p3_count reads the host im figure of a planted line, not its off figure" "$(yn [ "$(p3_count "$CTL_LINE" host im)" = 11 ])"
    check "control: p3_count reads the i686 off figure, not the digits of the target's name" "$(yn [ "$(p3_count "$CTL_LINE" i686 off)" = 20 ])"
    check "control: p3_count reads none for a label the line gives twice (i686 ci)" "$(yn [ "$(p3_count "$CTL_LINE" i686 ci)" = none ])"
    check "control: p3_count reads none for a label the line does not give (host cr)" "$(yn [ "$(p3_count "$CTL_LINE" host cr)" = none ])"
    printf '%s\n' '  f-host-off: exit 0; passed 10; failed 0; executed (passed plus failed) 10; test lines ending ok 10' \
      '  f-host-offx: exit 0; passed 11; failed 0; executed (passed plus failed) 11; test lines ending ok 11' \
      '  f-host-all: exit 0; passed 12; failed 0; executed (passed plus failed) 12; test lines ending ok 12' \
      '  f-host-all: exit 0; passed 13; failed 0; executed (passed plus failed) 13; test lines ending ok 13' \
      '  f-host-charts: exit 101; passed 14; failed 1; executed (passed plus failed) 15; test lines ending ok 14' >"$B/c-p3test"
    check "control: p3t_count reads 10 for f-host-off, not the figure of the longer name f-host-offx" "$(yn [ "$(p3t_count "$B/c-p3test" f-host-off)" = 10 ])"
    check "control: p3t_count reads none for a run the log gives twice (f-host-all)" "$(yn [ "$(p3t_count "$B/c-p3test" f-host-all)" = none ])"
    check "control: p3t_count reads none for a run that did not exit 0 with none failed (f-host-charts)" "$(yn [ "$(p3t_count "$B/c-p3test" f-host-charts)" = none ])"
    check "control: p3t_count reads none for a run the log does not give (f-host-images)" "$(yn [ "$(p3t_count "$B/c-p3test" f-host-images)" = none ])"
    check "control: same_count refuses 515 against 516" "$(ynnot same_count 515 516)"
    check "control: same_count refuses none against none" "$(ynnot same_count none none)"
    check "control: same_count takes 515 against 515" "$(yn same_count 515 515)"
    for name in ws-host ws-i686; do
      want_test=$(p3t_count "$B/p3-test.log" "$name")
      have=$(cat "$B/$name.passed")
      echo "  E $name: P3's test.log $want_test; this run passed $have"
      check "E $name: this run's passed count equals P3's test.log figure" "$(yn same_count "$have" "$want_test")"
    done
    for target in host i686; do
      for set in $SETS; do
        lab=$(p3_label "$set")
        want_gate=$(p3_count "$P3LINE" "$target" "$lab")
        want_test=$(p3t_count "$B/p3-test.log" "f-$target-$set")
        have=$(cat "$B/f-$target-$set.passed")
        echo "  E $target, features $set (P3's gate label $lab): P3's test.log $want_test; P3's gate.log $want_gate; this run passed $have"
        check "E $target, features $set: P3's test.log figure equals P3's gate.log figure" "$(yn same_count "$want_test" "$want_gate")"
        check "E $target, features $set: this run's passed count equals P3's figure" "$(yn same_count "$have" "$want_test")"
      done
    done
    plus_one=$(( $(cat "$B/f-host-off.passed") + 1 ))
    check "control: the same comparison refuses the host off run's count plus one" "$(ynnot same_count "$plus_one" "$(p3t_count "$B/p3-test.log" f-host-off)")"
    
    echo
    echo "## R: each F run's doc-tests result, 0 passed and 0 failed, and no crate source naming the README (change RC, kept; change UD)"
    # doc_result <cargo output>: "<passed> <failed>" of the first "test result:"
    # line after cargo's "Doc-tests emend" line, or "none". It prints, so its
    # value is compared.
    doc_result() {
      local r
      r=$(awk '/^[[:space:]]*Doc-tests emend[[:space:]]*$/ { d = 1; next }
        d && /^test result: / { p = 0; f = 0; for (i = 1; i < NF; i++) { if ($(i + 1) ~ /^passed;$/) p = $i; if ($(i + 1) ~ /^failed;$/) f = $i }; print p, f; exit }' "$1")
      if [ -n "$r" ]; then echo "$r"; else echo none; fi
    }
    printf 'test result: ok. 5 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored\n   Doc-tests emend\n\nrunning 1 test\ntest result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored\n' >"$B/c-doc1"
    check "control: doc_result reads 1 0 for a fixture with one doctest, not the lib line before it" "$(yn [ "$(doc_result "$B/c-doc1")" = "1 0" ])"
    printf 'test result: ok. 5 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored\n' >"$B/c-doc2"
    check "control: doc_result reads none for an output with no Doc-tests line" "$(yn [ "$(doc_result "$B/c-doc2")" = none ])"
    for target in host i686; do
      for set in $SETS; do
        dl=$(count_matches -E '^[[:space:]]*Doc-tests emend[[:space:]]*$' "$B/f-$target-$set.out")
        dr=$(doc_result "$B/f-$target-$set.out")
        echo "  R $target, features $set: Doc-tests emend lines $dl; its result (passed failed) $dr"
        check "R $target, features $set: one Doc-tests emend line, its result 0 passed 0 failed" "$(yn [ "$dl:$dr" = "1:0 0" ])"
      done
    done
    echo "command: git grep -l -F README <phase commit> -- crates/emend/src, counted; the same over crates/emend/Cargo.toml (the control: its readme line)"
    n_readme_src=$(git grep -l -F README "$C" -- crates/emend/src | count_lines /dev/stdin)
    n_readme_toml=$(git grep -l -F README "$C" -- crates/emend/Cargo.toml | count_lines /dev/stdin)
    echo "  the crate's source files naming README: $n_readme_src; crates/emend/Cargo.toml naming it: $n_readme_toml"
    check "R: no source file of the crate names the README, so the README commits compile nothing (change UD)" "$(yn [ "$n_readme_src" = 0 ])"
    check "control: the same count finds crates/emend/Cargo.toml's readme line" "$(yn [ "$n_readme_toml" = 1 ])"
    
    # clean_run <name>: exit 0 and no diagnostic line in the output.
    clean_run() { [ "$(cat "$B/$1.rc")" = 0 ] && [ "$(diag_lines "$B/$1.out")" = 0 ]; }
    # lint_run <name> <env...> -- <cargo args...>: runs cargo into <target>/main,
    # prints the exit and the diagnostic line count, writes <name>.rc.
    lint_run() {
      local name=$1 rc
      shift
      echo "command: CARGO_TARGET_DIR=<target>/main $*"
      env CARGO_TARGET_DIR="$CT" "$@" >"$B/$name.out" 2>&1 </dev/null
      rc=$?
      echo "$rc" >"$B/$name.rc"
      echo "  $name: exit $rc; diagnostic lines $(diag_lines "$B/$name.out")"
      LC_ALL=C "$G" -a -E "$DIAG" "$B/$name.out" | sed 's/^/    /'
    }
    echo 0 >"$B/c-lint.rc"; printf 'warning: unused variable\n    Finished x\n' >"$B/c-lint.out"
    check "control: clean_run refuses an exit-0 run with one warning line" "$(ynnot clean_run c-lint)"
    echo 0 >"$B/c-lint2.rc"; printf '    Checking x\n    Finished x\n' >"$B/c-lint2.out"
    check "control: clean_run takes an exit-0 run with none" "$(yn clean_run c-lint2)"
    
    echo
    echo "## C: clippy with -D warnings, the crate's six sets, the workspace and the size probe crate's three (changes UB and UE)"
    for set in $SETS; do
      # shellcheck disable=SC2207
      sa=($(set_args "$set"))
      lint_run "c-$set" cargo clippy -p emend --all-targets "${sa[@]}" -- -D warnings
      check "C features $set: exit 0, 0 diagnostic lines" "$(yn clean_run "c-$set")"
    done
    lint_run c-workspace cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings
    check "C workspace: exit 0, 0 diagnostic lines" "$(yn clean_run c-workspace)"
    PROBE_SETS="emend,xml charts images"
    for set in $PROBE_SETS; do
      lint_run "c-probe-$set" cargo clippy -p size-probe --all-targets --features "$set" -- -D warnings
      check "C size-probe, features $set: exit 0, 0 diagnostic lines (change UE)" "$(yn clean_run "c-probe-$set")"
    done
    
    echo
    echo "## D: rustdoc with -D warnings, the crate's six sets (change UB)"
    for set in $SETS; do
      # shellcheck disable=SC2207
      sa=($(set_args "$set"))
      lint_run "d-$set" RUSTDOCFLAGS='-D warnings' cargo doc -p emend --no-deps "${sa[@]}"
      check "D features $set: exit 0, 0 diagnostic lines" "$(yn clean_run "d-$set")"
    done
    
    echo
    echo "## the log itself"
    ALL=()
    for f in "$B"/*.out; do ALL+=("$f"); done
    nbytes=$(cat "${ALL[@]}" | LC_ALL=C tr -cd '\000-\010\013\014\016-\037\177\200-\377' | wc -c)
    printf 'x \033[31mred\033[m y caf\303\251\n' >"$B/ctl-bytes"
    nctl=$(LC_ALL=C tr -cd '\000-\010\013\014\016-\037\177\200-\377' <"$B/ctl-bytes" | wc -c)
    echo "  captured outputs: ${#ALL[@]}; control and non-ASCII bytes in them: $nbytes; in a control file with two ESC and one two-byte character: $nctl"
    check "no control or non-ASCII byte in any captured output" "$(yn [ "$nbytes" = 0 ])"
    check "control: the same count finds four bytes in the control file" "$(yn [ "$nctl" = 4 ])"
    echo "command: git status --porcelain"
    git status --porcelain | sed 's/^/    /'
    st_all=$(run_lines git status --porcelain)
    st_ours=$(run_matches -E "$OURS" git status --porcelain)
    echo "status lines: $st_all; of them this phase's logs or planning's spec: $st_ours"
    status_accounted() { [ "$st_all" != error ] && [ "$st_ours" != error ] && [ "$st_all" = "$st_ours" ]; }
    check "every change in the working tree is this phase's logs or planning's uncommitted spec" "$(yn status_accounted)"
    echo
    echo "## this script's own standard error (specs/0017 P1 verification ruling 7)"
    nerr=$(wc -c <"$ERR")
    cp "$ERR" "$B/c-err"; printf 'x' >>"$B/c-err"
    nerr_ctl=$(wc -c <"$B/c-err")
    echo "  command: wc -c <scripts>/out/test.stderr: $nerr bytes; a copy with one byte appended: $nerr_ctl"
    check "this script wrote nothing to its standard error" "$(yn [ "$nerr" = 0 ])"
    check "control: the same count reads one byte more in the copy" "$(yn [ "$nerr_ctl" = "$((nerr + 1))" ])"
    echo "run ended at $(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)"
    echo "test log checks all as expected: $([ "$fail" -eq 0 ] && echo yes || echo no)"
    exec 1>&3
    wait "$SCRUB_PID" 2>/dev/null
    exit "$fail"

## the shared counting library this log sources
    # emend shared counting forms (specs/0007-gate-self-test.md, "scripts/counting.sh").
    #
    # Sourced by scripts/gate-self-test.sh and by the log scripts of later phases,
    # which use these instead of carrying their own copy.
    #
    # WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. In T3 P4 four counting sites compared a bare `grep -c`
    # with 0 without reading the exit status, so a read error printed 0 and the
    # check passed. Two independent checks found them, and the class was declared
    # closed three further times while instances still stood: a helper whose first
    # repair read grep's status while `cat` was the failing command, and both
    # halves of one function in T2 P7. Planning's remedy is that every counting
    # form, including a throwaway one, is a named function that reads the exit
    # status and has a positive control, from the start.
    #
    # Every function prints either a number or the word "error". None of them ever
    # prints a matched line, a value, or a path. A caller that compares a result
    # with 0 therefore cannot mistake a failed read for an absence.
    #
    # counting_controls runs every form against a match, a miss and an unreadable
    # file, prints one line per control, and sets counting_controls_ok. Callers run
    # it before using any form.
    
    G=/usr/bin/grep
    
    # count_matches <-F|-E> <pattern> <file>...: total matching lines across the
    # files, or "error" if any file could not be read. The files go to grep
    # directly: with `cat file | grep -c`, an unreadable file makes CAT fail while
    # grep counts 0 over the empty stream and exits 1, so the read error reads as
    # "no hits".
    count_matches() {
      local mode=$1 pattern=$2 out rc
      shift 2
      out=$(LC_ALL=C "$G" -a -H -c "$mode" -e "$pattern" -- "$@" 2>/dev/null); rc=$?
      if [ "$rc" -ge 2 ]; then printf 'error'; return; fi
      printf '%s\n' "$out" | awk -F: '{ n += $NF } END { print n + 0 }'
    }
    
    # count_from_file <-F|-E> <pattern file> <file>...: the same, with the patterns
    # read from a file (grep -f), so a pattern is never printed.
    count_from_file() {
      local mode=$1 patterns=$2 out rc
      shift 2
      out=$(LC_ALL=C "$G" -a -H -c "$mode" -f "$patterns" -- "$@" 2>/dev/null); rc=$?
      if [ "$rc" -ge 2 ]; then printf 'error'; return; fi
      printf '%s\n' "$out" | awk -F: '{ n += $NF } END { print n + 0 }'
    }
    
    # count_bytes <tr class> <file>...: total bytes of that class across the files,
    # or "error" if any file could not be read. Each file is read on its own rather
    # than through `cat "$@" | tr`, for the same reason as above.
    count_bytes() {
      local class=$1 total=0 f n
      shift
      for f in "$@"; do
        [ -r "$f" ] || { printf 'error'; return; }
        n=$(LC_ALL=C tr -cd "$class" <"$f" | wc -c) || { printf 'error'; return; }
        total=$((total + n))
      done
      printf '%s' "$total"
    }
    
    # count_lines <file>...: total lines, or "error" if any file could not be read.
    count_lines() {
      local total=0 f n
      for f in "$@"; do
        [ -r "$f" ] || { printf 'error'; return; }
        n=$(wc -l <"$f") || { printf 'error'; return; }
        total=$((total + n))
      done
      printf '%s' "$total"
    }
    
    # count_matches_i <-F|-E> <pattern> <file>...: count_matches with -i, the form
    # the hygiene step uses for OFFICE_RE and ZONE_RE. It lived in a log script as a
    # hand-rolled copy with no control of its own (independent check 2, T6 P1, gap
    # 14); a counting form belongs here, with a control, even a throwaway one.
    count_matches_i() {
      local mode=$1 pattern=$2 out rc
      shift 2
      out=$(LC_ALL=C "$G" -a -H -c -i "$mode" -e "$pattern" -- "$@" 2>/dev/null); rc=$?
      if [ "$rc" -ge 2 ]; then printf 'error'; return; fi
      printf '%s\n' "$out" | awk -F: '{ n += $NF } END { print n + 0 }'
    }
    
    # run_lines <command...>: run the command, and print the number of lines it
    # wrote on stdout, or "error" if the command itself failed. The log scripts
    # asserted a clean working tree with
    #   git status --porcelain >file 2>/dev/null || : >file
    # which turns a FAILED git status into an empty file, so the check read a
    # failed command as a clean tree (independent check 2, T6 P1, gap 1; planning
    # reproduced it). Reading the command's exit status is the whole point: a
    # command that could not run is never the same as a command that found nothing.
    run_lines() {
      local out rc
      out=$("$@" 2>/dev/null); rc=$?
      if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ]; then printf 'error'; return; fi
      if [ -z "$out" ]; then printf '0'; return; fi
      printf '%s\n' "$out" | wc -l | tr -d ' '
    }
    
    # run_matches <-F|-E> <pattern> <command...>: the same, counting only the lines
    # that match, so a caller can compare "all lines" with "lines of the expected
    # shape" without either count hiding a failed command.
    run_matches() {
      local mode=$1 pattern=$2 out rc
      shift 2
      out=$("$@" 2>/dev/null); rc=$?
      if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ]; then printf 'error'; return; fi
      if [ -z "$out" ]; then printf '0'; return; fi
      printf '%s\n' "$out" | LC_ALL=C "$G" -a -c "$mode" -e "$pattern" || true
    }
    
    CTRL_CLASS='\000-\010\013\014\016-\037\177'
    NONASCII_CLASS='\200-\377'
    
    # counting_controls <label prefix>: run every counting form above against a
    # fixture that must match and one that must not, and against an unreadable
    # file, printing one line per result. Sets counting_controls_ok to yes or no.
    # This is the positive control planning requires for every form, and it also
    # proves each form reports "error" rather than 0 when it cannot read.
    counting_controls() {
      local d ok=yes r
      d=$(mktemp -d) || { counting_controls_ok=no; return; }
      printf 'alpha\nbeta needle gamma\nneedle\n' >"$d/hit"
      printf 'nothing of interest\n' >"$d/miss"
      printf 'x \033[31mred\033[m\017 y\ncaf\303\251\n' >"$d/bytes"
      printf 'needle\n' >"$d/patterns"
      printf 'unreadable\n' >"$d/locked"
      chmod 000 "$d/locked"
    
      r=$(count_matches -F needle "$d/hit");        echo "  control count_matches on a file with two matching lines: $r (expect 2)"; [ "$r" = 2 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_matches -F needle "$d/miss");       echo "  control count_matches on a file with none: $r (expect 0)"; [ "$r" = 0 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_matches -F needle "$d/hit" "$d/miss"); echo "  control count_matches summed over two files: $r (expect 2)"; [ "$r" = 2 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_matches -E 'ne+dle' "$d/hit");      echo "  control count_matches with a regular expression: $r (expect 2)"; [ "$r" = 2 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_matches -F needle "$d/locked");     echo "  control count_matches on an unreadable file: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_matches -F needle "$d/hit" "$d/locked"); echo "  control count_matches on readable plus unreadable: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_from_file -F "$d/patterns" "$d/hit"); echo "  control count_from_file on the same file: $r (expect 2)"; [ "$r" = 2 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_from_file -F "$d/patterns" "$d/miss"); echo "  control count_from_file on a file with none: $r (expect 0)"; [ "$r" = 0 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_from_file -F "$d/patterns" "$d/locked"); echo "  control count_from_file on an unreadable file: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_bytes "$CTRL_CLASS" "$d/bytes");    echo "  control count_bytes, control bytes in a file with ESC, ESC and SI: $r (expect 3)"; [ "$r" = 3 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_bytes "$NONASCII_CLASS" "$d/bytes"); echo "  control count_bytes, non-ASCII bytes in a file with one two-byte character: $r (expect 2)"; [ "$r" = 2 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_bytes "$CTRL_CLASS" "$d/miss");     echo "  control count_bytes on a clean file: $r (expect 0)"; [ "$r" = 0 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_bytes "$CTRL_CLASS" "$d/locked");   echo "  control count_bytes on an unreadable file: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_lines "$d/hit");                    echo "  control count_lines on a three-line file: $r (expect 3)"; [ "$r" = 3 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_lines "$d/locked");                 echo "  control count_lines on an unreadable file: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      printf 'NEEDLE\nneedle\n' >"$d/mixedcase"
      r=$(count_matches_i -F needle "$d/mixedcase"); echo "  control count_matches_i on mixed case: $r (expect 2)"; [ "$r" = 2 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_matches -F needle "$d/mixedcase");  echo "  control count_matches on the same file, case sensitive: $r (expect 1)"; [ "$r" = 1 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_matches_i -F needle "$d/locked");   echo "  control count_matches_i on an unreadable file: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      r=$(run_lines printf 'a\nb\nc\n');            echo "  control run_lines on a command printing three lines: $r (expect 3)"; [ "$r" = 3 ] || ok=no
      r=$(run_lines true);                          echo "  control run_lines on a command printing nothing: $r (expect 0)"; [ "$r" = 0 ] || ok=no
      r=$(run_lines false);                         echo "  control run_lines on a FAILING command: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      r=$(run_lines "$d/no-such-command");          echo "  control run_lines on a command that cannot run: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      r=$(run_matches -F needle printf 'needle\nother\n'); echo "  control run_matches on a command printing one match: $r (expect 1)"; [ "$r" = 1 ] || ok=no
      r=$(run_matches -F needle false);             echo "  control run_matches on a FAILING command: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
    
      chmod 644 "$d/locked"
      rm -rf "${d:?}"
      counting_controls_ok=$ok
      echo "  counting controls: $([ "$ok" = yes ] && echo "all as expected" || echo FAILED)"
    }

